# Every variable this service reads, and nothing else.
#
# Rule 9 of docs/ecosystem/03 §2: a repository declares the variables it needs and the deploy
# provides exactly those. `env_file: .env` fan-out — which hands every container the whole
# estate's secrets — is banned, and this file is what makes the alternative possible: the deploy
# manifest is derived from it.
#
# Copy to `.env` for local work. `.env` is gitignored and must stay that way.

# --- required --------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# There is NO database variable here, and there must never be one. This service owns no state:
# everything it serves is fetched from the service that owns it and held in an in-process cache
# with a stated TTL. A connection string here would be the first step towards a table holding a
# stale copy of a balance the ledger owns. CI fails on any *_DATABASE_URL in src/.

# Identity's JWKS and issuer. Token verification is the only thing this service asks identity for
# on the authentication path — the dashboard's identity reads go to IDENTITY_URL below.
IDENTITY_JWKS_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4001/.well-known/jwks.json
IDENTITY_ISSUER=http://127.0.0.1:4001

# The seven upstreams. Each is composed into one or more dashboard tiles, and each may be down
# without costing the page — see src/dashboard.ts.
LEDGER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4002
WALLET_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4003
IDENTITY_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4001
BILLING_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4004
ACTIVITY_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4005
PRICING_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4006
POLICY_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4007

# ONE long-lived credential, exchanged for SIX short-lived tokens — one per upstream, each narrowed
# to that peer's scope.
#
# THIS REPLACES HUB_LEDGER_TOKEN, HUB_WALLET_TOKEN, HUB_BILLING_TOKEN, HUB_ACTIVITY_TOKEN,
# HUB_PRICING_TOKEN and HUB_POLICY_TOKEN. All six were TOKENS — and a service token expires in 600
# seconds (identity/src/tokens.ts:28). They were read once at boot and nothing re-minted them, so
# ten minutes into every deployment all six died at once and every tile on the dashboard went
# `unavailable`. That was the widest blast radius of the defect anywhere in the estate, because
# this is the surface a user actually looks at.
#
# THE SEPARATION IS KEPT. AD-05: separating Hub from Nimbus "lets Hub's BFF hold a scoped service
# credential per upstream rather than being trusted with everything". A single whole-allowlist
# token would make a compromise of this process — the highest fan-out surface in the estate — a
# compromise of all seven peers at once. Identity reads the service off the credential ROW and
# never off the request, so one credential mints everything hub-api is allowed and the SCOPE SET is
# a request parameter, not a second secret. Six providers, six caches, six narrow tokens.
#
# The scope each one asks for, and nothing more — see UPSTREAM_SCOPES in src/upstreams.ts:
#   ledger      ledger:read
#   wallet      wallet:read      (never wallet:money — this service moves nothing)
#   billing     billing:read
#   activity    notify:read
#   pricing     pricing:read
#   policy      policy:decide    (the read side of it; this service never submits one)
#
# `deploy/scripts/estate-bootstrap.sh` writes it to compose/estate/tokens.env. It begins `cfsc_`;
# if what you have begins `eyJ` it is a token, not a credential, and the service refuses it at
# construction. A `cfsc_…` credential minted by identity — `deploy/scripts/estate-bootstrap.sh`.
# `src/env.ts` runs `@cloudsforge/secrets`' `assertServiceCredential`, which checks the prefix, 32
# decoded BYTES rather than 24 keystrokes, an entropy floor, and refuses a JWT BY NAME: pasting one
# of the six retired `HUB_*_TOKEN` values here would authenticate for ten minutes and then be the
# defect this variable replaced (micro-org #222). Empty is supported and means unconfigured.
#
# Technically optional — the image must be able to boot without one so CI can smoke-test /livez —
# but without it every tile is unavailable, and /readyz reports `identity-credential` as a hard
# failure so the replica takes no traffic.
HUB_API_IDENTITY_CREDENTIAL=

# Where the credential is exchanged. Defaults to IDENTITY_URL, which is already required above.

# There is no HUB_IDENTITY_TOKEN, and adding one would be a credential nobody can use.
# `GET /auth/me` and `GET /mfa/factors` are guarded by identity's `authenticateUser`, which refuses
# a service token outright. Identity is therefore called with the caller's own bearer, forwarded —
# see the header of src/upstreams.ts, and the gap in README.md that would remove the exception.

# --- optional, with the defaults shown ---------------------------------------------------------

PORT=4000
NODE_ENV=development
LOG_LEVEL=info

# The ceiling on a whole dashboard request. The fan-out is concurrent, so this is the budget for
# the *slowest* tile, not the sum of seven. Past it, whatever has not arrived becomes an
# `unavailable` tile with a reason and the 200 goes out anyway.
# HUB_DASHBOARD_DEADLINE_MS=2500

# The ceiling on one upstream call, retries included. Must not exceed the dashboard deadline, and
# boot refuses the pair if it does: an upstream allowed to outlive the page budget means no tile
# can ever reach its own deadline first, and the dashboard silently stops degrading.
# HUB_UPSTREAM_DEADLINE_MS=1500

# The per-upstream circuit breaker. After this many consecutive transport or 5xx faults the client
# stops calling that peer for HUB_CIRCUIT_RESET_MS, and the tile reports "circuit is open" instead
# of retrying for ever. A 4xx never trips it: that is the peer answering correctly.
# HUB_CIRCUIT_THRESHOLD=5
# HUB_CIRCUIT_RESET_MS=10000

# Names this replica in log lines. Defaults to the hostname, which is the container id under
# compose and the pod name under Kubernetes.
# INSTANCE_ID=

# The image tag, reported on every log line and in the release manifest.
# CLOUDSFORGE_TAG=dev

# --- not read by this service ------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT and the rest of OTEL_* are read by the OpenTelemetry SDK loaded ahead
# of the process (NODE_OPTIONS=--import @opentelemetry/auto-instrumentations-node/register), not by
# src/env.ts. They belong in the deploy, not here, because this service never reads them.
#
# The test suite reads nothing from the environment at all: it boots seven fake upstreams on
# ephemeral ports. `pnpm test` needs no configuration and no service running.
